# Line endings.
#
# The repository grew with mixed endings and no rule: 47 tracked source files
# with LF and 16 with CRLF, decided by whichever tool happened to write each
# one. That is not a style question, it is a correctness one. Editing a CRLF
# file with anything that writes LF turns a one-line change into a whole-file
# diff, which buries the actual edit and destroys blame for that file. It has
# happened here more than once, and once a search for a literal "\n" block
# silently matched nothing in a CRLF file and a scripted edit did nothing at
# all while reporting success.
#
# text=auto eol=lf: git normalises to LF in the repository and checks out LF
# everywhere. Node, Next and every tool in this project are indifferent to it,
# and Windows editors have handled LF for a decade.
#
# The commit that adds this also renormalises the 16 files that were CRLF.
# Their content is unchanged — only the line endings — but it is its own
# commit so the blame damage is isolated and explained rather than mixed into
# a change someone will later try to read.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, never touched.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ico binary
